# Line endings: LF in the repository, native in the working tree.
# Without this the result depends on each contributor's core.autocrlf setting,
# which is how a repository ends up with mixed endings inside the same file.
* text=auto

*.py   text diff=python
*.md   text
*.json text

# These keep LF even in a working tree on Windows. A Dockerfile with CRLF is a
# long-standing footgun - a line continuation inside RUN, or any shell script
# added later, breaks in ways that read as a build problem rather than a line
# ending problem - and YAML parsers are happier without the carriage returns.
Dockerfile     text eol=lf
.dockerignore  text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.yaml         text eol=lf
*.sh           text eol=lf

# A PKGBUILD is a bash script that Arch sources, and one carriage return in it
# produces errors that read as anything but a line-ending problem. .SRCINFO is
# parsed by the AUR and a .desktop by the desktop environment; neither has any
# business carrying CRLF just because it was last touched on Windows.
PKGBUILD       text eol=lf
.SRCINFO       text eol=lf
*.desktop      text eol=lf

# Never let line-ending normalisation near these. text=auto does detect binary
# content and leave it alone, but relying on detection for a file that has to
# survive byte-for-byte is a bad trade when saying so costs two lines.
*.ico          binary
*.png          binary
